.\" Manual page for makeneko
.TH MAKENEKO 1 "2026-05-22" "Neko" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
makeneko \- build a custom Neko executable with user-defined modules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B makeneko
.IR file1.f90 " [" file2.f90 " ...] [" file.cu " | " file.hip "]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B makeneko
is a build helper installed alongside
.BR neko (1)
that compiles and links one or more user-supplied source files against the
installed Neko library, producing a new executable called
.B neko
in the current working directory.
.PP
The script is used to build cases that need code beyond what the JSON case
file can express, for example custom initial conditions, boundary conditions,
source terms, or user-registered types and simulation components.
.PP
.B makeneko
inspects the supplied Fortran sources and automatically generates a small
driver,
.IR usr_driver.f90 ,
that
.IP \(bu 2
imports the special
.B user
module if one is present,
.IP \(bu 2
imports any additional user-defined modules found in the input files, and
.IP \(bu 2
invokes
.RI ${mod}_register_types()
for every user module that exposes such a routine, so that custom types
are registered with the Neko factories before
.B neko_init
is called.
.PP
The generated driver is compiled together with the user sources and the
installed Neko library using the Fortran compiler and flags chosen at the
time Neko was configured. After a successful build the temporary
.I usr_driver.f90
is removed.
.PP
If no
.B user
module and no type-registering modules are detected,
.B makeneko
refuses to build, since in that case the stock
.BR neko (1)
executable is sufficient.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IR file.f90 ", " file.F90
One or more Fortran sources containing user code. At least one is required.
.TP
.IR file.cu
CUDA source file. Accepted only when Neko was configured with the CUDA
backend; compiled with
.B nvcc
using the flags chosen at configure time.
.TP
.IR file.hip
HIP source file. Accepted only when Neko was configured with the HIP backend;
compiled with
.B hipcc
using the flags chosen at configure time.
.PP
Files with any other extension are ignored with a warning.
.SH USER MODULE
A source file may define a Fortran module named
.B user
that provides a
.B user_setup
subroutine. When detected, the generated driver calls
.B user_setup(C%user)
before
.BR neko_init ,
allowing the user to attach callbacks for initial conditions, boundary
conditions, forcing terms and similar hooks.
.PP
Type-registering modules are recognised by exporting a subroutine named
.RI ${module}_register_types ,
where
.I ${module}
matches the module name. These are called automatically by the generated
driver, unless the
.B user
module sets
.B C%user%suppress_type_injection
to
.BR .true. ,
in which case the user is responsible for performing the registration.
.SH OUTPUT
On success
.B makeneko
writes an executable named
.B neko
in the current directory. This binary is invoked exactly like the stock
.BR neko (1)
executable:
.PP
.RS
.B mpirun -n 8 ./neko mycase.case
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The user executable was built successfully.
.TP
.B 1
No source files were provided on the command line.
.TP
.B 2
No valid Fortran source files were given, or none of the supplied modules
contains a
.B user
module or a type-registering routine.
.TP
.B non-zero
The underlying Fortran, CUDA, or HIP compiler returned an error. The
diagnostic output of the compiler is shown on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I usr_driver.f90
Driver source generated by
.B makeneko
in the current directory. It is created at the start of the build and
removed once the build succeeds. Any pre-existing file with this name is
overwritten.
.TP
.I ./neko
Output executable produced in the current directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build a custom Neko binary from a single user file and run a case:
.PP
.RS
.B makeneko user.f90
.br
.B mpirun -n 4 ./neko mycase.case
.RE
.PP
Build with a separate module that registers custom simulation components:
.PP
.RS
.B makeneko user.f90 my_simcomp.f90
.RE
.PP
Build with an additional CUDA kernel (requires a CUDA-enabled Neko):
.PP
.RS
.B makeneko user.f90 my_kernel.cu
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR neko (1)
.PP
Project documentation and user guide:
.UR https://neko.cfd
.UE
